  "explains": "ReportsPage and StatementsPage change. ReportsPage imports list from the generated client (the operationId list of GET /reports) and also calls cache.get, an unrelated object whose method is called get; StatementsPage imports downloadStatement. An import of a function from the generated client is a resolved edge: the import says which function it is, so list() fits GET /v1/reports and downloadStatement(...) fits GET /v1/statements/{statementId}/download whatever the names are, and the handlers of those routes are system.py (list) and statements.py (download_statement, which imports app/services/render.py). The cap on generic and over-defined names is for a match by name alone, and cache.get is one: get is made only of generic words, so no member call is matched by it (a name match needs a distinctive name, and the cap counts definitions, not references), and cache.get is not a call of the operation get of GET /ping. downloadStatement is defined four times in the web app (the generated client and three unrelated functions in src/legacy), more than distinct_max = 3, and list and get are generic: the three names are recorded as not distinctive (not blocking), which says that no member call of them was matched. The change reaches the two screens through the routes files.",
